  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday) \nIssued at 112 AM MDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nA dangerous surge of monsoon moisture is forecast to produce \nwidespread heavy rainfall over the forecast area Tuesday through \nWednesday night with a very good chance for scattered occurrences\nof flash flooding, as well as some main stem river and tributary \nflooding. The aforementioned CA low pressure system and a mid-\nlevel high pressure system parked over eastern TX will draw very \nrich subtropical moisture mainly over southern, central, and \nespecially eastern parts of the forecast area; even as the upper \nlow opens into a trough while passing northeastward over the Great\nBasin, central Rockies, and northern Rockies. GEFS Ensemble PWATs\nspike 160-200% of normal by Tuesday through Wednesday night, with\nnear record (if not record) PWATs likely. The most likely rain \namount Tuesday through Wednesday night is currently forecast to be\n3-4\" from Ruidoso and Roswell northeastward to around Conchas \nLake; around 2-3\" elsewhere along and east of the central mountain\nchain and the lower Rio Grande Valley; around 0.5-1.5\" across the\nremainder of the Rio Grande Valley and continental divide; and \nlighter amounts west of the continental divide. The NBM 90th \nPercentile (serves as an upper-end scenario for planning purposes)\nindicates a 10 percent chance that some parts of the eastern \nplains will accumulate around 6-8\" of rain, with 4-6\" as far west \nas the central mountain chain and lower Rio Grande Valley, and \n2-3\" elsewhere in the Rio Grande Valley and along the continental \ndivide. \n\nThursday and Friday, the high is forecast to remain in place over\neastern TX, while another upper level trough moves onshore over \nthe Pacific northwest, drawing additional monsoon moisture and \nembedded disturbances northward over NM. Locally heavy rainfall\nwill continue to be a threat Thursday and Friday, especially given\nthe antecedent moisture by then, but rain amounts should trend\ndownward with each day. \n\nOn Saturday, scattered storms are still forecast, but the risk of\nlocally heavy rainfall looks to abate as drier air filters over \nthe forecast area from the west in response to the Pacific \nnorthwest trough exiting east of the Canadian Rockies. Models vary\non where tropical storms in the eastern Pacific will go during \nthe latter half of next week, but they generally agree that \nremnant moisture will remain south of NM.\n\nTemperature-wise, except for some above average readings over\neastern areas today, highs across the forecast area this week \nwill vary from a few to several degrees below 30-year averages \nwith the coolest readings on Wednesday.",
